\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task: Evidence-Grounded Document-Level Relation Extraction}
\label{sec:task}

Given a document $D = \{s_1, \ldots, s_n\}$ consisting of $n$ sentences and a set of entities $\mathcal{E}$ identified within $D$, the task of evidence-grounded document-level relation extraction (DocRE) requires predicting, for each entity pair $(e_h, e_t) \in \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{E}$, a relation $r \in \mathcal{R} \cup \{\texttt{NA}\}$ together with a set of supporting evidence sentences $E \subseteq D$. Standard DocRE predicts only the triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$; the evidence-grounded formulation also requires $E$, which grounds each prediction in specific textual support.

In our LLM-based approach, the model generates structured outputs that jointly encode both components. For each predicted relation, the output follows the format \texttt{(head, relation, tail) | Evidence: [S$i$][S$j$]}, requiring the model to produce complete evidence references inline with each triple rather than as a separate retrieval step. This design contrasts with encoder-based evidence methods such as SAIS~\citep{xiao2022sais} and DREEAM~\citep{ma2023dreeam}, which frame evidence identification as sentence-level binary classification. Because classification over a fixed sentence set does not increase output length, these methods incur no generation overhead. Our LLM formulation requires additional tokens for each evidence reference, creating a generation burden that scales with the number of predicted triples and their evidence sentences. This scale-dependent cost is the central phenomenon we investigate.


\subsection{Supervised Fine-Tuning Baseline}
\label{sec:sft}

We fine-tune all models with LoRA~\citep{hu2022lora} at rank $r{=}64$ and scaling factor $\alpha{=}128$, applied to attention projection matrices. The training data consists of DocRED~\citep{yao2019docred} training documents converted to the structured output format described above, where each example maps a document and its entity set to the complete set of relation triples with evidence annotations. We also train a \emph{no-evidence} variant under identical configuration but with evidence references removed from the output format, producing only \texttt{(head, relation, tail)}. This variant serves as an upper bound on extraction performance by eliminating the generation cost of evidence, and allows direct measurement of the evidence tax at each model scale. Detailed hyperparameters (learning rate, batch size, number of epochs) are reported in the Appendix.


\subsection{Optimization Paradigms}
\label{sec:paradigms}

We evaluate three post-SFT optimization paradigms that differ in how they incorporate quality signals from the CED scoring function (\S\ref{sec:ced}).

\paragraph{GRPO.}
Group Relative Policy Optimization~\citep{shao2024deepseekmath} is an online RL method that eliminates the need for a separate value network by computing advantages within groups of sampled outputs. For each training document, the policy generates $G$ candidate responses, which are scored by CED. The advantage of each response is computed relative to the group mean, and the policy is updated to increase the probability of above-average outputs. We test two configurations: QLoRA with $G{=}4$ groups under 4-bit quantization, and bf16 full-precision LoRA with $G{=}8$. Because GRPO applies gradient updates that directly optimize the reward signal, evidence quality and extraction accuracy compete for gradient capacity. We analyze this multi-objective tension in Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}.

\paragraph{DPO.}
Direct Preference Optimization~\citep{rafailov2023direct} reformulates RLHF as a classification problem over preference pairs, without explicit reward models. We construct preference data from the SFT model's sampled outputs: for each document, the response with the highest CED score is labeled as preferred and the lowest-scoring response as rejected. DPO then adjusts the policy to increase the log-probability ratio between preferred and rejected completions. We train for 1 and 3 epochs to observe the temporal dynamics of evidence-extraction trade-offs under implicit optimization.

\paragraph{RSFT.}
Rejection Sampling Fine-Tuning is a single-round variant of iterative self-improvement methods such as STaR~\citep{zelikman2022star}. The procedure is straightforward: the SFT model generates $N{=}8$ candidate outputs per training document, each candidate is scored by CED, and only the top-$k{=}1$ response is retained as training data. From 24,216 candidate outputs, this selection yields 9,042 training examples (37.3\% acceptance rate). Unlike GRPO and DPO, RSFT does not apply gradient-based optimization pressure toward a reward signal. Instead, it improves training data quality through filtering: the model trains on its own best outputs rather than all outputs. This distinction is critical because discrete selection over complete outputs cannot exploit the extraction-evidence trade-off that gradient methods find. The contribution of RSFT in our setting is not the framework itself, which is well-established, but its application to evidence-grounded DocRE with CED as a domain-specific quality filter.


\subsection{Contrastive Evidence Discrimination}
\label{sec:ced}

CED is a scoring function that evaluates the faithfulness of predicted evidence sentences to their associated relation triples. It builds on Natural Language Inference (NLI) to measure whether evidence actually entails the predicted relation, rather than merely co-occurring with the relevant entities.

For a predicted triple $(e_h, r, e_t)$ with evidence sentences $E$, CED constructs a positive NLI pair: the premise is the concatenation of sentences in $E$, and the hypothesis is a natural-language verbalization of the relation (e.g., ``$e_h$ has relation $r$ with $e_t$''). An NLI model (\texttt{cross-encoder/nli-deberta-v3-base}) produces an entailment probability $p^+$ for this pair. The contrastive component introduces hard negatives drawn from the same document. For each triple, we collect sentences that mention $e_h$ or $e_t$ but are not in the predicted evidence set. These entity-sharing distractors serve as negative premises, and the NLI model scores each to obtain $p^-_1, \ldots, p^-_m$. The CED score for a single triple is:
\begin{equation}
\text{CED}(e_h, r, e_t, E) = p^+ - \max_j \, p^-_j
\label{eq:ced}
\end{equation}
This formulation forces the scoring function to distinguish sentences that genuinely support the relation from sentences that merely mention the same entities. A high CED score indicates that the evidence provides relation-specific support beyond entity co-occurrence.

The document-level CED score aggregates over all predicted triples, weighted by extraction correctness: triples that match ground-truth relations contribute their CED scores, while incorrect triples receive zero credit. This coupling ensures that CED rewards both faithful evidence and accurate extraction jointly.

We also evaluate a \emph{flat-NLI} baseline that uses only the positive entailment score $p^+$ without the contrastive term. In our experiments (Section~\ref{sec:spectrum}), CED and flat-NLI produce comparable RSFT outcomes (mean Rel-F1 difference of 0.02pp across available seeds), and both outperform random selection and no-scoring baselines. We adopt CED as the default because the contrastive formulation provides interpretable per-triple discrimination scores that flat-NLI lacks.


\subsection{Evidence-Decoupling Ratio}
\label{sec:edcr}

We introduce the Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (EDCR) as a diagnostic metric that quantifies the degree of misalignment between a model's extraction and evidence capabilities. EDCR is not a training component; it serves purely as an analytical tool for comparing optimization methods.

EDCR is defined as:
\begin{equation}
\text{EDCR} = \frac{\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}}{\text{Rel-Recall}}
\label{eq:edcr}
\end{equation}
where $\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}$ is evidence sentence recall computed only over correctly predicted triples (true positives), and $\text{Rel-Recall}$ is overall relation extraction recall. The numerator captures evidence quality on the subset of triples the model gets right; the denominator captures how many triples the model identifies in total.

The intuition is as follows. A model that correctly identifies a few triples and provides excellent evidence for them, but misses most triples entirely, will have high $\text{Evi-Recall}_{\text{TP}}$ and low $\text{Rel-Recall}$, yielding a high EDCR. This pattern signals that evidence quality has decoupled from extraction capability: the model produces faithful evidence only within a narrow comfort zone.

EDCR operates at two diagnostic levels. At the method level, comparing EDCR across optimization paradigms reveals a strong negative correlation with Rel-F1 (Spearman $\rho{=}{-}0.965$): methods with higher EDCR produce worse extraction, confirming that elevated EDCR signals problematic decoupling. At the per-relation level, computing EDCR for individual relation types reveals variation that is independent of Evi-F1 ($\rho{=}{-}0.064$, $p{=}0.58$). This independence means EDCR captures relation-specific extraction-evidence misalignment that aggregate evidence metrics miss. The SFT baseline EDCR (0.6982) reflects the inherent cost of evidence generation; EDCR values substantially exceeding this level (e.g., GRPO QLoRA at 0.9876) indicate RL-induced decoupling beyond what evidence production alone explains, while values below the baseline (e.g., RSFT at 0.6521) reflect improved co-optimization.

\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

\paragraph{EDCR as a dual-layer diagnostic.}
The Evidence-Decoupling Ratio (\S\ref{sec:edcr}) provides diagnostic value at two complementary levels. At the method level, EDCR correlates strongly with extraction quality across optimization paradigms: Spearman $\rho = -0.965$ between EDCR and Rel-F1 (Table~\ref{tab:spectrum}). GRPO QLoRA reaches EDCR $= 0.9876$, confirming catastrophic decoupling; RSFT achieves $0.6521$ (seed 43), consistent with healthy joint optimization; SFT falls between at $0.6982$. The SFT baseline EDCR reflects the inherent cost of evidence generation; EDCR values substantially exceeding this baseline indicate RL-induced decoupling beyond what evidence production alone explains.

At the per-relation level, computing EDCR for each of the 76 relation types with $\geq$5 evaluation samples reveals a more nuanced picture. Per-relation EDCR correlates strongly with per-relation Rel-F1 ($\rho = -0.793$, $p < 0.001$), confirming its utility as a difficulty indicator for individual relation types. However, per-relation EDCR is statistically independent of Evi-F1 ($\rho = -0.064$, $p = 0.58$), and Evi-F1 itself shows no association with Rel-F1 ($\rho = -0.013$, $p = 0.91$). These two null correlations establish that evidence quality and extraction ability are fully decoupled across relation types: knowing how well a model extracts a given relation tells us nothing about its evidence quality for that relation. EDCR captures this misalignment precisely because it measures the \emph{ratio} of the two capabilities rather than either in isolation. Extreme cases illustrate the diagnostic power: \texttt{date\_of\_birth} has EDCR $= 0.118$ (highly faithful, with extraction and evidence tightly coupled), while \texttt{head\_of\_government} reaches $0.956$ (highly decoupled, where the model generates accurate evidence for a relation it rarely extracts correctly). Method-level EDCR thus serves as a horizontal comparison tool across optimization paradigms, while per-relation EDCR enables vertical diagnosis of which specific relation types suffer from extraction-evidence misalignment.


\paragraph{Why RSFT avoids decoupling.}
The structural difference between selection and optimization explains why RSFT does not exhibit the decoupling observed in gradient-based methods. GRPO and DPO update model parameters through gradients that optimize a reward signal. When two sub-tasks (extraction and evidence) compete for gradient capacity, the optimizer can discover and exploit the trade-off between them, improving one at the expense of the other. RSFT operates differently: it selects complete outputs and trains on them with standard supervised loss. Because the selection is discrete (accept or reject a full response), there is no gradient pathway through which the model can learn to sacrifice extraction for evidence quality. Only outputs where both extraction and evidence are jointly strong survive the CED filter. This property is not specific to RSFT's design but is a structural consequence of discrete selection over complete outputs: any selection-based method that filters on a joint quality criterion will inherit the same coupling guarantee. The implication for practitioners is direct: when optimizing for multiple coupled generation objectives, selection-based methods preserve inter-objective balance that gradient-based methods disrupt.


\paragraph{NLI scoring ablation.}
We compare CED (\S\ref{sec:ced}) against flat-NLI (positive entailment score $p^+$ only, without the contrastive term) as RSFT scoring functions. Across all available seeds, CED RSFT achieves mean Rel-F1 of 0.4563 (seeds 42, 43, 44, 45: range 0.4514--0.4621), and flat-NLI RSFT achieves mean Rel-F1 of 0.4561 (seeds 42, 43: range 0.4535--0.4586). The mean difference of 0.02pp suggests comparable performance; the small margin indicates that CED's contrastive design does not substantially outperform simpler NLI scoring for RSFT data selection. Both variants improve over the SFT baseline (Rel-F1 0.4049) by a wide margin, confirming that NLI-based scoring is effective regardless of the specific formulation. We adopt CED as the default because its contrastive formulation produces per-triple discrimination scores that support interpretable error analysis.

We also ablate the top-$k$ selection threshold. Best-of-8 ($k{=}1$) yields Ign-F1 45.00 and Rel-F1 0.4596, while top-3-of-8 ($k{=}3$) produces 43.64 and 0.4454. The selected training data confirms the quality gap: $k{=}1$ data has mean extraction F1 of 0.6157 and mean CED of 0.6683, versus 0.5761 and 0.6205 for $k{=}3$. Despite $k{=}3$ providing 2.96$\times$ more training examples, the additional lower-ranked samples introduce noise that degrades final performance. The critical factor for RSFT is scoring precision, not data volume.


\paragraph{Evaluation caveat.}
All evaluations in this work use exact-match F1, where entity mentions must match gold annotations precisely. As \citet{wan2024genres} observe, exact match systematically underestimates generative relation extraction: predictions that correctly identify the relation but use a different surface form for the entity are scored as incorrect. This penalty applies equally to all methods, so relative comparisons remain valid. However, absolute numbers (e.g., our 4B Ign-F1 of 45.12) likely understate the true extraction capability of LLM-based approaches. Developing evaluation protocols better suited to generative RE, such as fuzzy entity matching or embedding-based equivalence, remains an important direction for future work.
